  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 3NT. Only 5 top tricks are available (the ♠A, 2 heart tricks, the ♦A, and the ♣A). A successful finesse in either diamonds or clubs would provide enough tricks for the contract.\n\nAs East may eventually gain the lead, South should hold up the ♠A until the third round in an effort to exhaust East's spades. East's discard on the third round marks the 6-2 spade break.\n\nSouth should play on diamonds first, passing the ♦Q through West. It would be wrong to take the club finesse first. If West turns out to have the ♣K, the contract will fail immediately as West will cash his spades.\n\nWhen the diamond finesse loses, South's chances of making the contract are still good. South now has 8 tricks, but South should not take the club finesse for trick 9.\n\nSouth wins East's return and should guard against a singleton ♣K with West by cashing the ♣A, declining the finesse. If the ♣K does not drop, South will still succeed when East has the ♣K (the finesse would have also worked in this case). South loses only 4 tricks (2 spade tricks, the ♦K, and the ♣K).\n\nIf West was dealt the guarded ♣K, the contract could not have been made.",
